<% content_for :title, "Agent tools — crest" %>
<% content_for :description, "crest hands its tools to whatever AI agent is looking at the page, using the WebMCP browser standard." %>

<section style="padding: 30px 0 0;">
  <div class="cap" style="color: var(--oxblood);">WebMCP</div>
  <h1 class="display" style="font-size: 44px;">What an agent can do here</h1>
  <p class="lede" style="padding-top: 12px; max-width: 680px;">
    If you arrive with an AI agent, crest hands it a set of tools. Four read the record.
    Four work on the page in front of you &mdash; and you watch them happen.
  </p>
  <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; line-height: 2; color: var(--ink-soft); padding-top: 14px; max-width: 680px;">
    <strong style="color: var(--ink);">WebMCP</strong> is a browser standard that lets a website offer
    tools to whatever AI agent is looking at it &mdash; the way a page already offers buttons to a person.
    The agent does not scrape or guess. It is handed a named list of things this site can do, and it
    calls them in your browser, in your session.
  </p>
</section>

<section style="padding: 30px 0 0;">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="band">
      <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;">
        <%= render "shared/crest", width: 22, fill: "#f4f1e8", letter: "#14213d" %>
        <span class="cap">In your browser right now</span>
      </div>
      <span class="cap" data-model-context-target="label">checking&hellip;</span>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; line-height: 2; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0;">
        WebMCP is new, so most browsers do not have it yet. If this says the tools are not ready,
        the site still works exactly as normal &mdash; you just cannot hand it to an agent.
        Here is how to get a browser that can.
      </p>
      <div class="split" style="gap: 24px; padding-top: 20px;">
        <div>
          <div class="cap" style="color: var(--navy); padding-bottom: 8px;">ChatGPT&rsquo;s browser</div>
          <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.9; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0;">
            Works with no setup. The tools appear under <strong>Site tools</strong> in the address bar,
            and you can simply ask &mdash; &ldquo;take me to the 1994 cycle&rdquo;.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="cap" style="color: var(--navy); padding-bottom: 8px;">Chrome</div>
          <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.9; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0;">
            Enable <code>chrome://flags/#enable-webmcp-testing</code> and relaunch. Chrome has no agent
            built in, so you drive the tools yourself from the console.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="foot">
      Built for the <a href="https://openai.com/webmcp-challenge/">WebMCP Challenge</a>.
      The whole implementation is about a hundred lines &mdash;
      <a href="https://github.com/leemcalilly/crest">read it on GitHub</a>, MIT licensed.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding: 28px 0 0;">
  <hr class="rule">
  <div class="split" style="gap: 0; padding-top: 22px;">
    <div class="divider" style="padding-right: 34px; border-right: 1px solid var(--rule);">
      <h2 class="serif" style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 20px; margin: 0;">Why not just an API?</h2>
      <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; line-height: 2; color: var(--ink-soft); padding-top: 10px;">
        crest has an API &mdash; every page answers to <code>.json</code>. But an API can only
        <em>describe</em> this page. A tool registered in your browser can <strong>change it</strong>:
        move the timeline, light up a cycle, take you somewhere. You and the agent end up looking at
        the same thing at the same moment, which is the part a server cannot do.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="divided" style="padding-left: 34px;">
      <h2 class="serif" style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 20px; margin: 0;">Nothing here is agent-only</h2>
      <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; line-height: 2; color: var(--ink-soft); padding-top: 10px;">
        Every tool does something you can already do by clicking. crest sends no data anywhere,
        asks for no key, and costs nothing to run &mdash; the agent brings its own model. With no agent
        present the site behaves exactly as it always has.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <hr class="rule" style="margin-top: 24px;">
</section>

<section style="padding: 34px 0 0;" data-controller="clipboard"
         data-clipboard-text-value="<%= @full_prompt %>">
  <hr class="rule">
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: 20px; padding: 18px 0 10px; flex-wrap: wrap;">
    <h2 class="serif" style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 26px; margin: 0;">Try it yourself</h2>
    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 16px;">
      <span class="cap">One prompt &middot; eight steps</span>
      <button type="button" class="copy" data-action="clipboard#copy" title="Copy the whole test as one prompt">
        <svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
             stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
          <rect x="7" y="7" width="10" height="10" rx="1.5"></rect>
          <path d="M13 4.5H4.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 0 3 6v8.5"></path>
        </svg>
        <span data-clipboard-target="label">Copy the prompt</span>
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; line-height: 2; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0; max-width: 760px;">
    Copy the prompt and paste it into ChatGPT. It asks for a <strong>visible browser window</strong>,
    because otherwise the work happens somewhere you cannot watch &mdash; and watching the page move
    is the whole point. Everything below happens in the chart at the top of the site.
  </p>

  <ol class="tryit">
    <% @steps.each do |ask, expectation| %>
      <li>
        <span class="ask">&ldquo;<%= ask %>&rdquo;</span>
        <span class="saw"><%= expectation.html_safe %></span>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ol>

  <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; line-height: 1.9; color: var(--ink-faint); padding-top: 6px; margin: 0;">
    If the agent answers in words without the chart moving, it is describing the page rather than
    using its tools. Ask it to use the site&rsquo;s tools, or check the badge above says they are ready.
  </p>
</section>

<section style="padding: 26px 0 0;">
  <div class="cap" style="padding-bottom: 12px;">Tools that read the record</div>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <% @tools.select { it.kind == :read }.each do |tool| %>
        <tr>
          <td class="num" style="width: 190px; vertical-align: top; font-weight: 600;"><%= tool.name %></td>
          <td style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink-soft);"><%= tool.description %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section style="padding: 26px 0 0;">
  <div class="cap" style="padding-bottom: 12px; color: var(--green);">Tools that work on the page in front of you</div>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <% @tools.select { it.kind == :page }.each do |tool| %>
        <tr>
          <td class="num" style="width: 190px; vertical-align: top; font-weight: 600; color: var(--green);"><%= tool.name %></td>
          <td style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink-soft);"><%= tool.description %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <hr class="rule">
</section>
